title: 'Integrity Hardening Complete: The Numbers Don''t Lie'
summary: After building the integrity checker, we turned it on ourselves and fixed everything it found. Anchor resolution was producing 355 false positives — fixed by resolving relative to the .purpose file's directory. Duplicate detection was flagging portal/.purpose gate overlap — fixed with source-awareness. The orphan check was reporting 440 symbols when only 135 were truly isolated — fixed by distinguishing tree roots from true isolates. The system now informs without confusing.
symbols_touched:
  - '#IntegrityChecker'
  - '#integrity-command'
  - '#doctor-command'
  - ~anchor-resolution
  - ~advisory-first
decisions:
  - id: true-isolate-definition
    decision: Orphans = zero refs in AND zero refs out. Tree roots are not orphans.
    rationale: 440 reported orphans when 305 were structurally expected tree roots. A check that cries wolf teaches you to ignore it.
  - id: inform-not-confuse
    decision: Every number the system reports must be actionable
    rationale: False positives erode trust. 355 missing anchors that all exist, 3 duplicates that are by design, 440 orphans that are mostly features — each one trained the user to dismiss the check.
learnings:
  - Always run your checker on its own codebase before shipping — every assumption is tested immediately
  - Anchor paths are inherently relative to the file that declares them, not to the project root
  - Portal and .purpose gate overlap is by design — domain-aware dedup prevents false positives
  - The difference between a tree root and an orphan is whether the symbol has outgoing references — both have zero incoming, but only isolates have zero outgoing
